Answer /dev/harmony frames over a host socket in User-mode Linux

User-mode Linux has no doorbell port or MMIO window. With harmony_fd=<fd> the
kernel owns two ordinary pages for the request and response frames and rings
the doorbell by sending the virtual time and the request frame on the
inherited SOCK_SEQPACKET socket, then blocking for the response frame. The
host process answers while the guest is stopped, so the exchange takes no
virtual time. A failed exchange panics the guest. Without harmony_fd= the
driver registers no device.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a kernel diff under
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/patches/ per the repository's kernel-patch
exception.

